bb_pair: reconnect to the persisted peer automatically at boot

0005-*.patch persists a paired peer's MAC into the on-disk baseband
config so it survives the next boot's cfg_name= push -- but that alone
turns out not to be enough (confirmed on real hardware, both ground and
air units): the persisted candidate/ap_mac only tells the chip who it
is *allowed* to link with, it never makes the chip attempt the link by
itself. PRJ_CMD_EVENT_PAIR (BB_SET_PRJ_DISPATCH, same dispatch
_pair_thread_run already sends) is the only mechanism in this SDK that
moves a slot from BB_LINK_STATE_IDLE to LOCK/CONNECT. Nothing on the
firmware side re-issues it after a plain reboot, so every reboot still
needed ar8030-pair run by hand again, defeating the point of persisting
the peer in the first place.

Ported verbatim from the OpenIPC/builder air-side ar8030 package's
identical patch -- keep the two in sync.

Fix: S97ar8030 now spawns a detached background loop after the daemon
starts that keeps calling this tool (new --skip-if-connected
--no-persist flags below) until the link comes up, instead of blocking
boot on it (an air unit that isn't powered on yet must not delay the
rest of boot). It only runs at all when "<cfg>.paired" exists -- a new
marker file this patch has persist_paired_peer() write next to the
config on a successful pair. That gate matters: a never-paired unit
still carries the factory-default ap_mac, and on an AP-role config an
empty candidate list means "accept anyone" per the shipped config's own
"slot match all" help text, so looping this unconditionally from a
factory-default boot would have the unit silently re-broadcast
PRJ_CMD_EVENT_PAIR at whatever AR8030 happens to be in range -- pairing
for the first time has to stay the deliberate, manual ar8030-pair a
person runs once.

New flags on the tool itself:
  --skip-if-connected  do one immediate BB_GET_STATUS poll first; if a
                        peer is already connected, exit 0 without
                        touching pairing at all. Keeps the retry loop
                        from re-triggering PRJ_CMD_EVENT_PAIR against a
                        link that's already up (a manual ar8030-pair
                        run to bind to a *different* device still works
                        alongside it -- the daemon serializes dispatch
                        calls onto the one RPC connection, so the two
                        can't corrupt state, just queue).
Also: exit status now actually reflects whether a peer ended up
connected (0 connected / 1 not) instead of always 0, since the retry
loop's `while ! ar8030-pair ...; do sleep; done` depends on it -- this
changes interactively invoked runs too (previously a failed pair still
exited 0).

Not yet verified against real hardware: whether repeated
PRJ_CMD_EVENT_PAIR calls every ~10s indefinitely while a peer is absent
have any adverse effect on the chip (thermal, or upsetting a concurrent
manual pair attempt against a different peer); whether the "accept
anyone" reading of an empty AP candidate list is actually correct
instead of just "match all *already-known* slots" (in which case the
promiscuous-pairing concern above wouldn't apply, but the marker-file
gate is cheap enough to keep either way).

--- a/dev_helper/bb_pair/txg_bb_pair.cpp
+++ b/dev_helper/bb_pair/txg_bb_pair.cpp
@@ -351,16 +351,40 @@
         printf("persist: rename %s -> %s failed: %s\n", tmp_path, cfg_path, strerror(errno));
     }

+    // Marker for the boot-time auto-reconnect loop (see S60ar8030/S97ar8030):
+    // its whole point is to skip itself on a factory-default unit that has
+    // never been through a deliberate pair -- the default candidate/ap_mac
+    // is the placeholder 66000000/66000001 pair, and on the AP side an empty
+    // candidate list means "accept anyone" (see the config's own "slot
+    // match all" help text), so blindly auto-pairing before this file exists
+    // would let the loop silently bind to any nearby AR8030 in pairing
+    // range. Existence alone is enough for that check; content is just for
+    // a human reading it over ssh. Best-effort -- a failure here shouldn't
+    // undo an otherwise-successful persist.
+    if (ret == 0) {
+        char marker_path[520];
+        snprintf(marker_path, sizeof(marker_path), "%s.paired", cfg_path);
+        FILE* mf = fopen(marker_path, "w");
+        if (mf) {
+            fprintf(mf, "%s\n", mac_hex);
+            fclose(mf);
+        }
+    }
+
     cJSON_Delete(root);
     return ret;
 }

